"A theory of $q$-transversals (https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.12201)\nciteturn2academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.6 years ago; Authors: Mark Saaltink ... Given an indexed family ${\\cal A} = (A_1, A_2, \\dotsc, A_n)$ of subsets of some given set $S$, a \\emph{transversal} is a set of distinct elements $x_1, x_2, \\dotsc, x_n$ with each $x_i \\in A_i$. ... In this paper we define a $q$-analog of the theory of transversals, and show that many of the main properties of ordinary transversals are shared by this analog.\nTitle: A theory of $q$-transversals\nAuthors: Mark Saaltink\nDate: Sat Mar 15 16:45:55 2025\n\nGiven an indexed family ${\\cal A} = (A_1, A_2, \\dotsc, A_n)$ of subsets of some given set $S$, a \\emph{transversal} is a set of distinct elements $x_1, x_2, \\dotsc, x_n$ with each $x_i \\in A_i$. Transversals have been studied since 1935 and have many attractive properties, with a deep connection to matroids. A $q$-analog is formed by replacing the notion of a set by the notion of a vector space, with a corresponding replacement of other concepts. In this paper we define a $q$-analog of the theory of transversals, and show that many of the main properties of ordinary transversals are shared by this analog.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEvery q-transversal matroid is representable over a finite extension | trureturing (https://www.trureturing.org/research/saaltink-2025-q-transversal-representability/)\nciteturn2search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 6 days ago; Saaltink, A theory of q-transversals, arXiv:2503.12201v1, Section 4.2, page 14, states: ... | Item | Status | Evidence | | --- | --- | --- | | Conjecture 3 settlement and canonical mathematical assertion | Proved | Kernel-checked `QTransversalRepresentability.result` | | Retained witness: one specialization preserves all required generic minors | Proved | Kernel-checked private `generic_minor_nonzero` and `specialized_independent_subspaces` | | Mechanism: annihilating rows and simultaneous specialization | Proved | Kernel-checked private `specialized_independent_subspaces` and `qRank_eq_imageRank`; the route above describes their composition | | Finite extension specified by polynomial degrees | Proved | Kernel-checked private `simultaneous_specialization_fin` and `finite_extension_primitive_degree`, with the choice described below | | Uniform product bound $\\prod_i(\\operatorname{codim}X_i+1)$ and smaller sufficient degrees | Open | No delivered Lean statement proves this bound or optimality; the general finite-extension assertion does not assert it | | Coordinate-family case of Theorem 24 | Proved in prose | Specialize `result` to coordinate subspaces; its quantifier over arbitrary families includes that subclass | | Source Conjecture 1, the q-analogue of Rado's theorem | Open | Separate source question; the classical linear Rado lemma here is not Conjecture 1 | | Source Conjecture 2, uniqueness of minimal presentations | Open | No delivered statement addresses presentation uniqueness | | Definition 14 versus Theorem 18 in the sampled families | Computed | The experiment above: six $(3,3)$ families and three $(4,2)$ families, all their subspaces, exit 0, stated immutable script and SHA-256 | | Rank equality for the sampled explicit matrices | Computed | The same experiment and scope, with extension degrees 6 through 24, exit 0 | | Mathematical novelty and publication priority | Open | Literature reading: linear Rado and Kronecker substitution are established methods; this is a formal proof of the stated conjecture, with literature completeness `ASSUMED-UNVERIFIED` |\n\n# Every q-transversal matroid is representable over a finite extension\n\nLiterature status: not rechecked Repository record: proved\n\nResolution source: D5/S3/Combinatorics/Matroids/QTransversalRepresentability.result10.48550/arXiv.2503.12201Source dossierRelease 36a49830636b\n\nProblem\n\n## The question\n\nM. Saaltink, A theory of q-transversals, arXiv:2503.12201v1, Section 4.2, page 14, states:\n\n> Conjecture 3. All q-transversal matroids are representable.\n\nThe source note records Definition 14, Theorem 15 and the representation convention of Section 3.2. The preregistration is #19965. The settling declaration is `D5/S3/Combinatorics/Matroids/QTransversalRepresentability.result`.\n\n### Statement echo\n\n| Source clause | Lean counterpart | Fidelity | | --- | --- | --- | | Finite field $\\mathbb F_q$ | `F`, `Field F`, `Fintype F` | Arbitrary finite field | | Finite family of subspaces of a finite-dimensional vector space | `m`, `k`, `X : Fin k → Submodule F (Fin m → F)` | Coordinates identify the ambient space with $F^m$ | | Every basis of a partial q-transversal admits a partial avoiding transversal | `IsPartialQTransversal` | Every independent finite spanning set admits an injective assignment to indices, with each vector outside its assigned subspace | | Rank of the q-transversal matroid | `qTransversalRank` | Supremum of dimensions of partial q-transversals contained in the argument subspace | | Section 3.2 matrix rank | `representedRank` | Rank of the representing matrix times the transpose of a basis-coordinate matrix | | One representing matrix for the whole q-matroid | `claim`, `result` | One extension and one matrix give equality of the two ranks for every subspace | | A field extending $F$ | `Algebra F K`, `FiniteDimensional F K` | A finite-dimensional extension strengthens the source requirement |\nThe private `restricted_annihilator_hall` supplies the deficit Hall inequalities for the restricted annihilators. `D5.S3.Combinatorics.Matroids.LinearRado.spanning_representatives` then supplies a spanning selection, and `D5.S3.Combinatorics.Matroids.LinearRado.exists_nonsingular_rows` extracts a nonzero minor. The private `generic_minor_nonzero` establishes such a minor for each partial q-transversal.\n\nThere are finitely many subspaces over the finite base field. The private `simultaneous_specialization` preserves the finite family of selected nonzero minor polynomials in one finite extension. The private `specialized_independent_subspaces` therefore gives one linear map, vanishing on each assigned $X_i$ coordinate, whose images are independent on every partial q-transversal.\n\nConversely, `D5.S3.Combinatorics.Matroids.LinearRado.independent_support_matching` turns independent image vectors into an avoiding assignment. The private `imageRank_attained` finds a subspace attaining the image rank, and `qRank_eq_imageRank` identifies the two rank functions. The private `representedRank_matrixOfLinear` expresses the image rank as the required matrix rank. `result` combines these statements.\n\nTriage\n\n## Scope assessment\n\n`QTransversalRepresentability.result` proves Conjecture 3 for arbitrary finite subspace families over a finite base field. The four-slot escape audit is unfinished under #22087; the sharp uniform extension-degree bound remains open.\n\n| Item | Status | Evidence | | --- | --- | --- | | Conjecture 3 settlement and canonical mathematical assertion | Proved | Kernel-checked `QTransversalRepresentability.result` | | Retained witness: one specialization preserves all required generic minors | Proved | Kernel-checked private `generic_minor_nonzero` and `specialized_independent_subspaces` | | Mechanism: annihilating rows and simultaneous specialization | Proved | Kernel-checked private `specialized_independent_subspaces` and `qRank_eq_imageRank`; the route above describes their composition | | Finite extension specified by polynomial degrees | Proved | Kernel-checked private `simultaneous_specialization_fin` and `finite_extension_primitive_degree`, with the choice described below | | Uniform product bound $\\prod_i(\\operatorname{codim}X_i+1)$ and smaller sufficient degrees | Open | No delivered Lean statement proves this bound or optimality; the general finite-extension assertion does not assert it | | Coordinate-family case of Theorem 24 | Proved in prose | Specialize `result` to coordinate subspaces; its quantifier over arbitrary families includes that subclass | | Source Conjecture 1, the q-analogue of Rado's theorem | Open | Separate source question; the classical linear Rado lemma here is not Conjecture 1 | | Source Conjecture 2, uniqueness of minimal presentations | Open | No delivered statement addresses presentation uniqueness | | Definition 14 versus Theorem 18 in the sampled families | Computed | The experiment above: six $(3,3)$ families and three $(4,2)$ families, all their subspaces, exit 0, stated immutable script and SHA-256 | | Rank equality for the sampled explicit matrices | Computed | The same experiment and scope, with extension degrees 6 through 24, exit 0 | | Mathematical novelty and publication priority | Open | Literature reading: linear Rado and Kronecker substitution are established methods; this is a formal proof of the stated conjecture, with literature completeness `ASSUMED-UNVERIFIED` |\n\n### What the settlement shows\n\nProved (kernel-checked): one finite-dimensional extension $K/F$ and one $k\\times m$ matrix represent the q-transversal rank on every subspace, for every finite base field and arbitrary finite subspace family. The decisive mechanism is simultaneous preservation of selected generic minors, followed by the converse avoiding-matching argument and attainment of image rank.\n\nProved (kernel-checked construction, without a sharp degree formula): let $P$ be the finite nonzero minor family and let $Q$ be its univariate Kronecker images. The proof sets $D=1+\\max_{q\\in Q}\\deg q$, with the finite supremum equal to zero for the empty set, and uses `FiniteField.Extension F p D` with an element of minimal-polynomial degree $D$. The weights are the mixed-radix prefix products of support-coordinate bounds. This describes the actual extension construction; it does not identify $D$ with the product bound in #19965.\n\nProved in prose (scope): the arbitrary-family conclusion includes the coordinate-family assertion of Theorem 24. Any source argument assuming only Conjecture 3's representation assertion can use this conclusion; no other source theorem is independently reproved here. Conjectures 1 and 2 and optimal extension degrees remain open.\n\nASSUMED-UNVERIFIED\n\n## Unverified assumptions\n\nLiterature-search completeness and publication priority are unverified beyond the screen recorded in #19965. The compiled proof establishes the literal rank representation claim, not a sharp universal extension-degree bound. The four-slot escape audit is unfinished under #22087. The finite experiment does not establish a uniform theorem beyond its stated sampled families.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA theory of $q$-transversals (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/389917054_A_theory_of_q-transversals)\nciteturn2search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.7 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; Mark Saaltink ... In this paper we define a q-analog of the theory of transversals, and show that many of the main properties of ordinary transversals are shared by this analog. ... With this function we conjecture a q-analog of Rado’s theorem (The- ... If M is a q-transversal matroid of rank r, then it has a presen-\n\nis independent iﬀ for all B≤A we have f(B)≥dim B. The rank of an element\n\nA is the maximum of dim B for any independent B≤A. An explicit formula\n\nfor the rank function is\n\nr(A) = min\n\nB≤A f(B) + dim A−dim B.\n\nWhen q-matroids M and M′are both deﬁned on the same lattice L(V), we\n\nwrite M∨M′for the union of q-matroids as described in [2]. That is, we deﬁne\n\nfunction f:L(V)→Z by the equation f(A) = r(A) + r′(A) for all A≤V; then\n\nf is submodular, and M∨M′is the q-matroid it induces. It is easily shown\n\nthat in determining (M 1∨M 2)∨M 3, we can just use one step and induce from\n\nf(A) = r 1(A) + r 2(A) + r 3(A), and similarly for larger numbers of summands.\n\nPropositio