sis of T is a partial\n\navoiding traversal of X, and so T is a partial q-transversal.\n\nThe proof clearly shows an analog of Theorem 6\n\nCorollary 16. A q-matroid is q-transversal iﬀ it is the union of a collection of\n\nrank 1 q-matroids.\n\nWe say “collection” here rather than “set” as there may be multiple copies\n\nof a q-matroid needed.\n\nWe also have a result corresponding nicely to Hall’s (Theorem 1), in its\n\navoidance version Corollary 9.\n\nTheorem 17. System X= (X 1, X 2,...,X n)has a q-transversal iﬀ for every\n\nnonempty J⊆ {1. . n}we have\n\ndim X(J) + |J| ≤ dim V.\n\nProof. Following the line of argument in the proof of Theorem 15, deﬁne rank\n\nfunctions r i and function f. Then the matroid of partial q-transversals of X is\n\ninduced by f and its rank function is therefore\n\nr(V) = min\n\nT≤V f(T) + dim V−dim T.\n\nr(V) can be at most n; if we take T=V we see r(V)≤f(V)≤n. So X has a\n\nq-transversal iﬀ f(V)≥n, equivalently iﬀ for all T≤V we have\n\nf(T) + dim V−dim T≥n.\n\nFor any T we have f(T) = n− | {i|T≤X i} |, as each r i adds 1 to f(T) unless\n\nT is a subspace of X i. Now let J={i|T≤X i}. Then we have T≤ X (J) so\n\nthat dim T≤dim X(J) and f(T) = n− | J|. So we have\n\nf(T) + dim V−dim T≥n− | J|+ dim V−dim X(J),\n\nwith equality when T equals X(J), and this is always larger than or equal to n\n\nunder the conditions stated in the theorem.\n\nCorollary 7 in its avoidance form Theorem 11 also has a q-analog.\n\nTheorem 18. A subspace T is a partial q-transversal of family X= (X 1,...,X n)\n\niﬀ for every J⊆ {1. . n}we have\n\ndim (T∩ X (J)) + |J| ≤ n\n\nProof. Let M be the q-matroid of partial q-transversals of X, and let r be\n\nits rank function. A calculation similar to the proof of Theorem 17 shows\n\nr(T) = dim T is equivalent to the given condition.\n\n10\n\nWe deﬁne bar nullity as an analog of co-nullity that will not require us to\n\ntake a dual. If M is a q-matroid and B is the set of bases, then for X≤V we\n\ndeﬁne\n\nn(X) = min